00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:20.361
This work is about Laura and

00:43:20.361 --> 00:43:22.460
what we should be aware of when we're using Laura.

00:43:23.581 --> 00:43:25.661
So to motivate us, Laura is

00:43:25.661 --> 00:43:27.741
of course everywhere. It's the go to method that

00:43:27.741 --> 00:43:29.931
you probably use whenever you're fine tuning

00:43:29.991 --> 00:43:32.181
a model. However, we

00:43:32.181 --> 00:43:34.441
haven't studied very much spurious correlations

00:43:34.441 --> 00:43:36.521
and the fact under explored in real

00:43:36.521 --> 00:43:38.751
world data sets. Which are rarely clean.

00:43:39.871 --> 00:43:41.891
So things like leftover markup, metadata,

00:43:42.111 --> 00:43:44.291
templates can all actually end up

00:43:44.531 --> 00:43:46.841
having correlations in the fine tune model.

00:43:47.081 --> 00:43:49.221
That Laura can latch onto. This

00:43:49.221 --> 00:43:51.401
leads to shortcut behavior that makes models

00:43:51.461 --> 00:43:53.731
unreliable and

00:43:53.731 --> 00:43:56.051
worse if used by malicious actor that can be

00:43:56.051 --> 00:43:57.981
injected into the dataset.

00:43:58.701 --> 00:44:00.861
And so that's what we study here in this work.

00:44:03.661 --> 00:44:05.920
So to do this, the method proposed

00:44:05.921 --> 00:44:08.021
is called Seamless Spurious Token

00:44:08.081 --> 00:44:10.201
Injection. And the idea is

00:44:10.201 --> 00:44:12.660
to have a controlled framework to inject

00:44:12.801 --> 00:44:14.341
spurious correlations into the dataset.

00:44:14.871 --> 00:44:16.951
An example is one that you see up here on

00:44:16.951 --> 00:44:18.971
the top right. Which is injecting a little h

00:44:18.971 --> 00:44:20.561
one tag for HTML.

00:44:21.701 --> 00:44:23.910
And while this looks innocuous, if it's

00:44:24.151 --> 00:44:26.241
correlated at all with the class label that you're trying

00:44:26.721 --> 00:44:28.801
find tune your model on, the model ends up learning this

00:44:28.801 --> 00:44:30.821
as a shortcut doesn't actually learn what

00:44:30.821 --> 00:44:31.951
it is that you care about.

00:44:33.521 --> 00:44:35.681
The core result, I'm gonna kinda highlight

00:44:35.681 --> 00:44:38.000
four things. One is that a single spurious token

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:40.411
is actually enough to dominate the predictions of

00:44:40.411 --> 00:44:42.477
the model and prevent it from learning the pattern that

00:44:42.477 --> 00:44:44.941
you do care about. Two,

00:44:45.401 --> 00:44:47.451
there's a kind of a non monotonic relationship

00:44:47.691 --> 00:44:49.881
between the lower rank, so higher rank amplifiers.

00:44:51.091 --> 00:44:52.601
Even with light corruptions.

00:44:53.480 --> 00:44:55.641
So there's no single safe rank that you can

00:44:55.641 --> 00:44:57.530
choose. And then third,

00:44:57.690 --> 00:45:00.091
the effect persists across token types, position,

00:45:00.091 --> 00:45:01.600
model size, or training duration.

00:45:02.161 --> 00:45:04.321
Even a 24,000,000,000 parameter model is

00:45:04.321 --> 00:45:06.470
vulnerable. So it doesn't just go

00:45:06.470 --> 00:45:07.871
away with scale. And then fourth,

00:45:08.511 --> 00:45:10.591
and maybe most alarmingly, the author said

00:45:10.591 --> 00:45:12.750
grammar checkers paraphrases

00:45:12.911 --> 00:45:15.151
cannot remove these spurious correlations if they're in the

00:45:15.151 --> 00:45:17.111
dataset. So the bottom line is

00:45:17.271 --> 00:45:19.531
lower efficiency comes with an understudied robustness

00:45:19.561 --> 00:45:21.581
trade off, Spares correlations are

00:45:21.581 --> 00:45:23.585
a core part of what we should be

00:45:23.585 --> 00:45:25.831
thinking about when we're using LoRa. So be aware of that.

